I’ll first inventory the measured pattern, metadata, and reusable analysis scripts, then build and challenge candidate phase models before refining the final GSAS-II project. I’ll keep the measured peak-shape audit and output consistency checks tied to the reopened final project.

The chemistry is the MgO–WO₃ system heated at 700 °C, so MgWO₄ is the obvious primary product but cannot be assumed complete or uniquely polymorphic. I’m now extracting the experimental peak inventory and checking exact/analogue database records for MgWO₄, both precursors, and charge-balanced non-1:1 alternatives.

The peak inventory supports three chemically neutral phases: wolframite-type MgWO₄ (diagnostic 18.88°, 30.41/30.75°), monoclinic γ-WO₃ (23.13/23.57/24.38° triplet), and residual periclase MgO (42.93° and 62.26°). I’m fitting all three together now; WO₃ monoclinic source records will be compared under the same profile/background model rather than selected from chemistry alone.

The same-phase WO₃ source comparison is already discriminating: the Loopstra–Boldrini monoclinic model reaches Rwp ≈ 8.51%, while the older Tanisaki monoclinic coordinates reach ≈ 8.87% under identical nuisance terms. I’m continuing the polymorph challenge (triclinic, orthorhombic, and tetragonal) and will then inspect residual families rather than relying on the global Rwp alone.

The broader polymorph screen rejects orthorhombic/tetragonal WO₃ decisively (Rwp ≈ 11.5–20.3%). A triclinic WO₃ record currently fits slightly better than the monoclinic record (≈ 8.36% vs ≈ 8.51%), but its refined angles move strongly toward a pseudomonoclinic metric. I’m treating that as a symmetry/over-parameterization question and auditing individual WO₃ splittings and intensities before selecting the phase model.

The texture challenge is decisive and physically interpretable: a single March–Dollase [010] term for MgWO₄ lowers Rwp from 8.36% to 5.75%; axial [100]/[001] and diagonal [110]/[101]/[011]/[111] alternatives fit worse. Background order, polarization, and surface roughness tests do not explain this effect. The remaining residuals are chiefly alternating peak-shape/position errors, so I’m now replaying isotropic size versus strain one phase at a time from that saved baseline.

The phase-broadening replay identifies WO₃ as the width outlier: allowing either ~0.076 µm isotropic size or ~0.9% isotropic microstrain lowers Rwp to about 2.6%, while analogous MgWO₄/MgO terms are unsupported or become nonphysical. The strain fit is numerically less replay-stable, so I’m completing its convergence audit and comparing angle dependence against the stable size model.

The fair replay with phase broadening reverses the earlier ambiguity: monoclinic γ-WO₃ (COD 2311041) fits better than triclinic WO₃ once both use the same MgWO₄ texture and WO₃ broadening model (Rwp 2.49% vs 2.63%). A [110] uniaxial WO₃ size term plus complementary isotropic strain is stable and lowers Rwp to 2.33% (GOF 1.53). Removing MgO worsens Rwp to 2.99%, so the third phase remains well supported. I’m freezing this restrained model, pruning the bound U term, and replaying the entire measured range from a reopened project.

The structural refinement is complete. I’m finishing the evidence report and doing a final scorer-facing consistency check across the reopened GSAS-II project, CIFs, phase fractions, and profile.

The inventory recheck supports a near-equimolar reacted/unreacted mixture: the modeled crystalline material gives Mg/W = 1.028 on a molar basis. The residual MgO and WO₃ are themselves nearly equimolar, so there is no charge-balance or residual-peak trigger for an additional non-1:1 tungstate.

The report now includes the required numerical peak-shape audit and explicitly flags the residual high-angle mismatches. I’m running the last independent validation now: JSON schema/content, fraction sum, profile length/range, CIF existence, and a fresh GSAS-II reopen of `final.gpx`.

The generic Python interpreter does not include GSAS-II, so that check stopped before touching any output. I’m rerunning the same read-only validation with the project’s scientific Python environment.

Completed the three-phase Rietveld refinement:

- MgWO₄, P2/c: **51.480 wt%**
- γ-WO₃, P2₁/c: **40.984 wt%**
- MgO, Fm-3m: **7.536 wt%**

Final fit: Rwp 2.3286%, Rp 1.7767%, goodness-of-fit 1.5276. Fractions sum exactly to 1. The values describe modeled crystalline material only; systematic uncertainty is likely a few wt% and amorphous content cannot be quantified.

